# A foreach head binds ONE name. `for k, q in xs:` used to fall through to the
# condition form, which parsed `k` as the condition and died "expected ':'
# before the block" with the caret on the comma -- a message that names neither
# the rule nor the fix (FRICTION.md #12, where the author met this refusal and
# then a second one, `p[0]`, before finding the body-destructure form).
#
# The diagnostic now spells the working form out, substituting the names that
# were written: "a `for` binds one name -- write `for p in COLL:` then
# `k, q := p` in the body". Refused in the parser (src/tychoc.c@TK_COMMA scan in
# the TK_FOR handler), so this file must not compile.
#
# The second binder is NOT reserved for an index here, which is what both
# neighbour languages spend it on -- measured 2026-08-13: Go's `for a, b := range
# xs` binds a=index, and Odin's `for v, i in xs` binds i=index (int), so neither
# destructures the element either.
fn main():
    xs := [("a", 1), ("b", 2)]
    for k, q in xs:
        println(k + str(q))
